text:
anish_r: Anybody seen this? My edge function on Vercel errors with `TypeError: (0 , _inference.createClient) is not a function` even though local node test works. Using require() in a CJS project.

anish_r: Minimal handler: 
```js
// api/edge.js
const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference');
module.exports = async (req, res) => {
  const c = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  res.json({ ok: true });
};
```

anish_r: Deploys fine, but runtime throws that TypeError. Locally `node api/edge.js` calls fine.

liz_docs: That error usually means the module shape is different at runtime (common when package.json has an "exports" map with only an "import" entry). Vercel edge uses native ESM resolution so it might be loading the module differently than your local CJS resolver.

omar_b: yup. We had a support case: package.json has something like:
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
  }
}
```
If the "require" target is missing or the bundler/runtime ignores it, require() can end up getting the ESM module wrapper which doesn't expose named exports in the same shape.

anish_r: Ah — package.json in node_modules/@redwood/inference has only "import" target. That explains it. Any workaround without changing our whole project to ESM?

liz_docs: Workarounds:
- Use dynamic import in your edge handler: `const { createClient } = await import('@redwood/inference');` (works on ESM-only packages)
- Create a tiny ESM wrapper file that re-exports named exports and import that wrapper from your CJS code via dynamic import or by running a small build step
- Pin to a release that includes a CJS entry (we should keep one if possible)

omar_b: For TS consumers: `esModuleInterop` toggles how default vs named imports behave. If your TS config is strict and you `require()` an ESM-only package, types won't line up. Add a note in docs: "If you use CJS `require()` and experience `is not a function`, try dynamic import or enable `esModuleInterop`/`allowSyntheticDefaultImports` to improve compatibility."

anish_r: Dynamic import worked in a quick test (swapped handler to async and used `await import()`), but I'm worried about cold-start latency and bundler behavior on Vercel. Any measured perf hit?

liz_docs: It can add a tiny I/O step on first run but usually negligible compared to model calls. Still worth mentioning. We'll add a short sentence: "dynamic import may add a small one-time startup cost on cold starts."

build-bot: Note: added internal ticket INF-724 to track publishing a CJS fallback and ensuring the package exports map contains both import and require targets.

anish_r: Thanks — could we get a short docs snippet that lists the common import forms and the expected runtime compatibility? People keep mixing `import sdk from` vs `import { createClient }` vs `const { createClient } = require(...)`.

liz_docs: Draft snippet I'll add to quickstart: 
```js
// Preferred (ESM, edge-friendly)
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference';

// Also works (default alias)
import createClientDefault from '@redwood/inference';
const clientA = createClientDefault({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// CJS fallback (only if package supports require())
const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference');

// CJS + edge workaround (dynamic import)
module.exports = async (req, res) => {
  const { createClient } = await import('@redwood/inference');
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  res.json({ ok: true });
};
```

liz_docs: I'll open a docs PR and link the support ticket and the internal INF-724. Will mark the quickstart compatibility section with a small table of runtimes + recommended import pattern.

omar_b: Also flagging bundlers: esbuild can rewrite `require()` to ESM imports depending on target. Add a pointer to bundler notes.

anish_r: Great — thanks for the quick help. Will try the dynamic import for now and report if anything else pops up.

build-bot: Docs PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/418 (compatibility + import examples).
